---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import BlowupLaboratory from "../components/visuals/BlowupLaboratory.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Finite-Time Blowup — Visuals by Zuhaib Ul Zamann"
  description="An interactive model of the collapsing vortex in OpenAI's September 2026 finite-time blowup construction for the three-dimensional Navier–Stokes equations."
>
  <section class="experiment-head">
    <div>
      <a href="/visuals" class="label back">← Visual laboratory</a>
      <p class="label label--accent">Experiment 010 · Fluid singularities</p>
      <h1>Finite-Time<br />Blowup</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      In September 2026 OpenAI published a construction in which a
      three-dimensional fluid, starting from rest, reaches unbounded velocity in
      finite time while its kinetic energy stays bounded. This is the flow: a
      vortex drawn into an ever more slender column, and the oscillations that
      keep the force driving it smooth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <BlowupLaboratory />

  <section class="brief">
    <div>
      <p class="label">What is being claimed</p>
      <p>
        Fefferman's statement of the Millennium problem offers four
        alternatives. (A) and (B) ask you to prove that smooth solutions always
        exist; (C) and (D) ask you to exhibit a breakdown, and permit a smooth
        external force. The paper's Theorem 1.1 settles (C), and its corollary
        on the torus settles (D): for every viscosity ν &gt; 0 there is a force
        f ∈ C<sub>c</sub><sup>∞</sup> and a smooth solution on ℝ³ × [0, 1)
        starting from u(·, 0) = 0 with
        sup<sub>t</sub> ‖u(t)‖<sub>L²</sub> &lt; ∞ and
        limsup<sub>t↑1</sub> ‖u(t)‖<sub>L<sup>∞</sup></sub> = ∞. It does not
        settle the unforced alternatives (A) and (B), and at the time of
        writing the proof is under review and its authorship is disputed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="label">How the flow works</p>
      <p>
        Fluid spirals inward toward the axis and is thrown out along it, on
        opposite sides of a dividing layer near z = 0. Radial inflow carries
        angular momentum r·u<sub>θ</sub> inward and spins the core up; the axial
        jet clears the arriving fluid so the inflow never chokes. The core
        contracts self-similarly with radius ℓ<sub>r</sub> ≍ τ<sup>½</sup> and
        height ℓ<sub>z</sub> ≍ τ<sup>½−h</sup>, so it becomes an increasingly
        slender column. Sustaining that background alone would need a force that
        blows up in the annulus around it, so the construction adds oscillatory
        ring pulses: seeded exponentially small, amplified by the background
        shear, and eventually killed by viscosity as the shear steepens their
        radial wavenumber. Their averaged momentum fluxes cancel the singular
        part of the residual, leaving a force that extends smoothly through
        t = 1.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="source-note">
    <span class="label">Provenance</span>
    <p>
      Geometry, similarity coordinates, scaling exponents and the pulse
      mechanism follow
      <a href="https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/32d9f210-8b73-45e0-91bc-82a30aef8a9a/navier-stokes.pdf"
        >“Finite time blowup for Navier–Stokes”, OpenAI, September 2026</a
      >
      (see also the
      <a href="https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/">announcement</a>).
      The profiles E, U and Ψ animated here are illustrative stand-ins for the
      ones the paper constructs, chosen so that the leading radial pressure
      balance ∂<sub>r</sub>p = u<sub>θ</sub>²/r holds exactly and the exterior is
      an Oseen–Lamb vortex, an exact solution of the radial heat equation for
      u<sub>θ</sub>. The anisotropy is exaggerated whenever h is pushed past
      1/100, as the paper's own Figure 1 exaggerates it. This page is a
      visualisation, not a verification: the formal Lean development is
      OpenAI's.
    </p>
  </footer>
</Base>

<style>
  .experiment-head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr minmax(19rem, 0.68fr);
    align-items: end;
    gap: 3rem;
    padding: 3rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(3.2rem, 8.5vw, 6.5rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 0.85;
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 31rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.2rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .brief {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .brief p:not(.label) {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .source-note {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
  }

  .source-note p { max-width: 43rem; line-height: 1.75; }

  @media (max-width: 44rem) {
    .experiment-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; }
    .brief { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.75rem; }
    .source-note { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
